from <math> import exp, sqrt, pnjlim, jexp, vcrit_of, q_dep;

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  Junction Diode — SPICE Level-1 (Berkeley) model
//
//  What this buys over the Shockley equation it replaces
//  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  The old model was id = Is*(e^(vd/nVt) - 1) with a fixed 2 pF across the
//  terminals. That curve is right over maybe four decades in the middle of the
//  forward region and wrong everywhere else. Added here, in the order they
//  start to matter:
//
//    Rs    Ohmic series resistance. Above a few tens of mA this is what
//          actually sets the forward drop — the exponential has gone nearly
//          vertical and every further millivolt is I*Rs. Without it a 1N4001
//          reaches 1 A at about 0.85 V instead of the 1.1 V on its data sheet.
//    Isr   Recombination current in the depletion region, with its own
//    Nr     emission coefficient (typically ~2). This dominates the forward
//          current BELOW roughly a microamp, and it is why a real diode's
//          Gummel slope is 120 mV/decade near the bottom and 60 mV/decade in
//          the middle rather than 60 throughout.
//    Ikf   High-injection knee. Beyond it the injected carrier density rivals
//          the doping and the slope halves, id ~ e^(vd/2nVt).
//    Bv    Reverse breakdown, as an exponential turn-on at -Bv carrying Ibv.
//    Ibv    This is what makes a Zener a Zener; the old model would have
//          conducted nothing at -5 V and clamped anything past -100 V into a
//          numerically dead region.
//    Cjo   Depletion charge from the real junction law rather than a fixed
//    Vj     capacitor: 22 pF at 0 V is not 8 pF at -10 V, and reverse recovery
//    M      depends on the difference.
//    Fc
//    Tt    Transit time — the DIFFUSION charge, stored minority carriers. This
//          is reverse recovery. A switching diode has Tt in picoseconds and a
//          rectifier has it in microseconds, and that single number is most of
//          why you cannot use a 1N4001 in a switching supply.
//
//  Convergence: the solver is real Newton, so keep derivatives alive
//  ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  vm_compute_jacobian (runtime/vm/vm.cpp) builds a FULL numerical Jacobian by
//  perturbing every node voltage by sqrt(eps)*max(|v|,1), re-running the analog
//  blocks and measuring how B_static moved. The solvers consume it with
//  Jacobian reuse and Newton damping. It is true Newton-Raphson — a
//  finite-difference Jacobian instead of an analytic one, but structurally what
//  SPICE does.
//
//  The consequence is the single most important thing about this file: the
//  derivative the solver uses comes FROM THIS SOURCE. Whatever slope the code
//  has is the slope Newton gets — and note that this is a property of the
//  derivative being taken from the model at all, not of it being taken by
//  finite differences. Automatic differentiation would report the same slopes.
//  Everything below about keeping derivatives alive would survive unchanged if
//  the Jacobian became analytic tomorrow. So the previous model's
//
//      if (vd > 0.85)   { vd = 0.85; }
//      if (vd < -100.0) { vd = -100.0; }
//
//  was not a physical bound and not a safety net — it was a region of exactly
//  zero derivative, i.e. a zero column in the Jacobian, a direction the solver
//  cannot step along. The comment claiming those clamps "represent the literal
//  physical limits of a standard PN junction" had it backwards twice over: a
//  real junction has no such wall (past 0.85 V it keeps conducting, which is
//  what Rs is for, and past -100 V it breaks down, which is what Bv is for),
//  and the wall actively caused divergence rather than preventing it. Removing
//  the identical clamp from the BJT models is what fixed the amplifier cases
//  that had been failing there. There are no voltage clamps below.
//
//  What keeps the iteration in bounds instead:
//    · pnjlim() limits the STEP, not the value — the Berkeley junction
//      limiter, fed the previous NR iterate via nr_prev(). It never flattens
//      the curve; it only refuses to leap across it in one bound.
//    · jexp() guards overflow on the exponential's argument, out at |x| = 40
//      where nothing physical is left. See <math>'s jexp() for why that one hard
//      clamp is tolerable and why smoothing it made things worse.
//    · GMIN, a 1 pS conductance in parallel with the junction, exactly as
//      SPICE does it. In deep reverse bias every exponential term has gone
//      flat and the branch would otherwise present a truly singular row; this
//      keeps the conductance strictly positive everywhere. 1 pS is 100 pA at
//      100 V, far below any real leakage.
//
//  The one thing to know before using it: cold starts want .op
//  ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//  A transient that begins with the diode ALREADY deep in forward conduction
//  will fail to find its operating point at t = 0 UNLESS the deck declares
//  `.op;`. Measured on a 1N4148 into 1 kOhm without it: a source starting at
//  1 V or 2 V is fine, 3 V and above is not. With `.op;` the same 5 V start
//  solves exactly — 0.6514 V across the junction at 4.349 mA, flat from the
//  first timestep.
//
//  Sources that begin at or near zero and ramp are unaffected either way,
//  which is why the examples using this model do not all carry `.op;`.
//
//  The cause is specific, and it is narrower than it first looks. It is not
//  the exponential current, not pnjlim (removing it entirely changes nothing),
//  and not ddt itself. It is that the DIFFUSION charge Tt*Is*e^(v/nVt) has a
//  derivative — a capacitance — that varies exponentially with voltage.
//
//  Bisected at a 5 V cold start into 1 kOhm, holding the capacitance at the
//  solution fixed near 1 nF in all three:
//
//    diffusion charge through ddt()          fails at t = 0
//    a LINEAR 1 nF charge through ddt()      converges, 2048 timesteps
//    a LINEAR 1 nF as a C<> primitive        converges, 2021 timesteps
//
//  So the synthesized charge element is fine and the magnitude is fine. What
//  the solver cannot do is start at v = 0, where this capacitance is
//  essentially zero, and reach v = 0.65, where it is a nanofarad, while the
//  element's stored charge goes from nothing to full in the same instant —
//  every trial point along the way has a capacitance orders of magnitude
//  different from the last.
//
//  SPICE does not hit this because it always solves a DC operating point
//  first. Hover's equivalent is opt-in: without `.op;` the transient starts
//  from a zero-state guess, which for this element is not a consistent one.
//  vm_solve_op (runtime/vm/vm.cpp) does source stepping — it ramps the
//  independent sources through twelve alphas with a damped iteration — and
//  that is enough to walk the diffusion charge up to its correct value.
//
//  One caveat when reading its log: vm_solve_op prints "OP alpha=... complete"
//  unconditionally, including when its 500-iteration inner loop ran out
//  without converging. The line means the alpha was attempted, not that it
//  succeeded, so do not take a clean-looking OP log as proof of a solved
//  operating point — check the first few rows of output instead.
//
//  Not modelled: temperature (Vt is passed in, so this is an isothermal model
//  — the card's Eg/Xti scaling is absent), and the Level-3 refinements.
//
//  Parameters are ordinary SPICE card values with their usual positive signs.
//  Rs must be > 0: R<0> is a short and the primitive rejects it, so parts use
//  1 mOhm where the card specifies none. Ikf, Isr, Bv, Cjo and Tt are each
//  disabled by passing 0.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

analog module Diode<double Is,  double N,
                    double Rs,
                    double Ikf,
                    double Isr, double Nr,
                    double Bv,  double Ibv,
                    double Cjo, double Vj, double M, double Fc,
                    double Tt,
                    double Vt>()
    [wire anode, wire cathode]
{
    // Internal anode. The junction equations are written across ai-cathode,
    // and the ohmic drop from the terminal to the junction is carried by Rs —
    // the same split as the BJT's intrinsic nodes.
    wire ai;

    R<Rs>() [anode, ai];

    double nvt   = N  * Vt;
    double nvt_r = Nr * Vt;

    // Per-iteration junction limiting. nr_prev() reaches back to the previous
    // NEWTON iterate (not the previous timestep), which is what a junction
    // limiter needs: it bounds how far one iteration may travel up the
    // exponential, then lets the next iteration continue from there.
    double vd = pnjlim(V(ai) - V(cathode), nr_prev(V(ai) - V(cathode)),
                       nvt, vcrit_of(Is, nvt));

    // ── Ideal diffusion current ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    double i_fwd = Is * (jexp(vd, nvt) - 1.0);

    // ── High-injection knee ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Beyond Ikf the injected minority density approaches the doping and the
    // effective slope halves. sqrt(Ikf/(Ikf+i)) is 1 well below the knee and
    // falls as 1/sqrt(i) above it, which is exactly that halving.
    //
    // No guard on the denominator: i_fwd is bounded below by -Is, so
    // Ikf + i_fwd >= Ikf - Is, and any physical card has Ikf many orders above
    // Is. A guard here would be a zero-derivative branch bought for a case
    // that cannot occur.
    double kinj = 1.0;
    if (Ikf > 0.0) {
        kinj = sqrt(Ikf / (Ikf + i_fwd));
    }

    // ── Depletion-region recombination ──────────────────────────────────────
    // The low-current tail, with its own emission coefficient (~2).
    double i_rec = Isr * (jexp(vd, nvt_r) - 1.0);

    // ── Reverse breakdown ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Turns on exponentially below -Bv and passes exactly -Ibv at vd = -Bv,
    // which is how a card specifies the pair. Above -Bv the argument is deeply
    // negative and jexp floors it, leaving a term of order 1e-18*Ibv — nothing
    // next to Is, so the off-state is unaffected.
    //
    // It gets its OWN pnjlim, on its own argument, and it has to. Breakdown is
    // a second exponential with a second turn-on point, so a step that is
    // modest for the forward junction can be enormous for this one: a first
    // iterate at -8 V on a 5.1 V part lands 2.9 V up an e^(v/Vt) curve, which
    // is e^111 before any limiting. Reusing the forward limiter does not help,
    // because it is watching a different voltage. Treating -(Bv + vd) as the
    // forward voltage of a diode whose saturation current is Ibv makes the
    // same limiter apply verbatim.
    //
    // The V()-differences are respelled rather than reusing vd: nr_prev()
    // rewrites the V() leaves inside its argument, so it has to be handed the
    // node expression, not a local that was already limited.
    double ibv_eff = Ibv;
    if (ibv_eff <= 0.0) { ibv_eff = 1.0e-12; }   // keeps vcrit_of finite

    double vbd = pnjlim(-(Bv + (V(ai) - V(cathode))),
                        nr_prev(-(Bv + (V(ai) - V(cathode)))),
                        Vt, vcrit_of(ibv_eff, Vt));

    double i_bv = 0.0;
    if (Bv > 0.0) {
        // NOT jexp() here, and the difference is not cosmetic. jexp guards its
        // argument at BOTH ends, and the lower guard floors the exponential at
        // e^-40 ~ 4.2e-18. For the forward current that floor is harmless — it
        // just yields the correct -Is. For this term it manufactures a constant
        // -Ibv * 4.2e-18 that is present at every bias, in every diode, forever:
        // about -4e-23 A on a 10 uA Ibv. The true breakdown current a thousand
        // volts short of Bv is around 1e-190, i.e. zero.
        //
        // That spurious constant is not negligible in practice. Substituting a
        // literal -4.25e-23 A for this whole term reproduces the failure of a
        // full-bridge rectifier exactly, while removing the term lets the same
        // circuit solve in 3418 steps — so this floor, and not breakdown
        // physics, is what a blocking diode was contributing.
        //
        // Only the upper guard is needed. IEEE exp() underflows to zero
        // gracefully on the way down, with no exception and no denormal
        // trouble, so the off-state term is exactly 0 and contributes exactly
        // nothing — which is both the physically right answer and the
        // numerically quiet one.
        double x_bd = vbd / Vt;
        if (x_bd > 40.0) { x_bd = 40.0; }
        i_bv = -Ibv * exp(x_bd);
    }

    // ── Total branch current ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // GMIN closes the loop on the Jacobian: see the header. It is SPICE's
    // default 1e-12 S and is deliberately not a parameter, because tuning it
    // per part would be tuning the solver, not the device.
    double i_d = i_fwd * kinj + i_rec + i_bv + 1.0e-12 * vd;

    current_source<>(i_d) [ai, cathode];

    // ── Charge storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Diffusion charge (stored minority carriers, ~ Tt * conducted current)
    // plus depletion charge. ddt() rewrites to a 1 F capacitor driven by a
    // current source, so the derivative comes out of the MNA solve itself
    // rather than from a difference taken here — which is what makes the
    // capacitance correctly bias-dependent instead of frozen at its DC value.
    // The depletion term is built from the RAW terminal voltage, not from the
    // limited vd, and that difference is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
    //
    // SPICE evaluates its capacitance at the limited voltage and gets away with
    // it because limiting there is TRANSPARENT TO THE JACOBIAN: $limit (and
    // SPICE's pnjlim before it) moves the operating point only, and dq/dv is
    // taken with respect to the unlimited junction voltage. Hover has no such
    // hook — pnjlim() is an ordinary function in this block's data flow, so its
    // own slope is part of the derivative. Note this is NOT about the Jacobian
    // being finite-difference rather than analytic: pnjlim's limiting branch
    // has derivative 1/(arg - 2) with respect to v_new, and automatic
    // differentiation would report exactly the same value. Any faithful
    // differentiation of the code as written divides the capacitance by
    // whatever the limiter is doing to the step. Where the limiter is
    // compressing hardest — a first iterate several volts up the curve, which
    // is exactly a transient started at full bias — its local slope is a few
    // parts per thousand, and the junction presents essentially NO capacitance
    // at the one moment the solver most needs some. That was reproducibly
    // fatal: an operating point at 5 V through 1 kOhm failed to converge with
    // the limited voltage here and converges with the raw one.
    //
    // Nothing is lost by using the raw voltage. The depletion law is a mild
    // power law with no overflow to guard against, and limiting exists only to
    // tame the exponential. The diffusion term below keeps the limited current,
    // because that IS the exponential — and at convergence dv is small, pnjlim
    // passes through untouched, and the two agree exactly.
    double q_d = Tt * i_fwd * kinj
               + q_dep(V(ai) - V(cathode), Cjo, Vj, M, Fc);
    double i_c = ddt(q_d);

    current_source<>(i_c) [ai, cathode];
}
